{"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","modules":[{"name":"Math"}],"title":"Custom Dimensions and Units"},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CustomDimensions","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/math\/customdimensions"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math"]]},"kind":"article","abstract":[{"text":"Extend ","type":"text"},{"code":"Math","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with your own physical dimensions and units—currency, points, gameplay metrics, or anything else your app measures.","type":"text"}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"generated":true,"anchor":"Extending-the-Framework","title":"Extending the Framework","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CompileTimeSafety"]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Math"},{"text":" ships with a comprehensive set of base dimensions (","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/length","isActive":true},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/time","isActive":true},{"text":", and friends) and derived dimensions (","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/speed","isActive":true},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/energy","isActive":true},{"text":", and more). These cover physics and everyday measurement, but your app almost certainly measures things that aren’t in the SI system too: money, hit points, ingredients, resource points, users, and so on.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The good news: ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension","isActive":true},{"text":" is an open system. Because a dimension is ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"just a dictionary of exponent powers","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", you can describe any dimension you can imagine at runtime.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Define a Custom Dimension","anchor":"Define-a-Custom-Dimension","level":3,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Create a ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension","isActive":true},{"text":" with a dictionary of exponent identifiers. Each key is a dimension name and each value is its power:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["import Math","","\/\/ A dimension for money, with a single base exponent.","let usdDimension = PhysicalDimension(exponents: [\"USD\": 1])","","\/\/ A dimension for \"users\", used later in per-user metrics.","let userDimension = PhysicalDimension(exponents: [\"user\": 1])"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"You can build compound custom dimensions with the same arithmetic used","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"internally: ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"+"},{"text":" multiplies dimensions, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"-"},{"text":" divides them, and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"*"},{"text":" with an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"text":" raises a dimension to a power:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["let revenuePerUser = usdDimension - userDimension   \/\/ [\"USD\": 1, \"user\": -1]"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"text":"Define Custom Units","anchor":"Define-Custom-Units","level":3,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Custom dimensions don’t have a compile-time type, so use","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/MathDimension\/unknown","isActive":true},{"text":" as the type-level parameter and ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/NamedUnit","isActive":true},{"text":" to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"create units. Pass a "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/LinearConverter","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" (for a simple scale) or an"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/OffsetConverter","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" (for a scale plus an offset) to handle conversions:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["let usdUnit = NamedUnit<MathDimension.unknown>(","    symbol: \"$\",","    dimension: usdDimension,","    converter: LinearConverter(coefficient: 1.0)",")","","\/\/ EUR is defined relative to the same base dimension.","let eurUnit = NamedUnit<MathDimension.unknown>(","    symbol: \"€\",","    dimension: usdDimension,","    converter: LinearConverter(coefficient: 1.09)",")"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"type":"aside","name":"Tip","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Keep the same ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PhysicalDimension"},{"text":" value (here ","type":"text"},{"code":"usdDimension","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") for every","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"unit in a family. The framework only converts between units whose dimensions","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"match, so sharing the exact same value makes your money interchangeable.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"style":"tip"},{"anchor":"Convert-Between-Custom-Units","text":"Convert Between Custom Units","type":"heading","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Custom units convert exactly like the built-in ones:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["let walletInEur = Quantity(value: 100.0, unit: eurUnit)","let walletInUsd = walletInEur.converted(to: usdUnit)","","print(walletInUsd.value) \/\/ 109.0"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"level":3,"anchor":"Combine-Custom-Units-with-Built-In-Ones","text":"Combine Custom Units with Built-In Ones","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Custom dimensions participate in dimensional arithmetic too. Here a currency","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"dimension combines with the built-in "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/time"},{"type":"text","text":" dimension:"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let hourlyRate = Quantity(value: 50.0, unit: usdUnit) \/ Quantity(value: 1.0, unit: Units.hour)","","print(hourlyRate.value)          \/\/ 50.0","print(hourlyRate.unit.dimension) \/\/ [\"USD\": 1, \"time\": -1]","print(hourlyRate.unit.symbol)    \/\/ \"($\/h)\""]},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Use-the-Currency-Dimension","level":3,"text":"Use the Currency Dimension"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"If your custom dimension happens to be currency, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Math"},{"text":" already defines a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/currency"},{"text":" dimension and a built-in library of world","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"currencies (and cryptocurrencies) in "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/Units"},{"text":". Currency units automatically","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"place their symbol before the value when formatting:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let gift = Quantity(value: 100.0, unit: Units.usd)","print(gift.formatted()) \/\/ \"$100.00\"","","\/\/ Resolve units from ISO 4217 codes at runtime.","if let currency = Units.currency(for: \"SEK\") {","    print(currency.symbol) \/\/ \"kr\"","}"]},{"level":3,"text":"What’s Next?","type":"heading","anchor":"Whats-Next"},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Learn how to lock custom units into a compile-time dimension in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CompileTimeSafety"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Explore "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/Units"},{"type":"text","text":" to see the breadth of units provided out of the box."}]}]}]}],"kind":"content"}],"references":{"doc://Math/documentation/Math/CompileTimeSafety":{"url":"\/documentation\/math\/compiletimesafety","kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CompileTimeSafety","type":"topic","title":"Compile-Time Dimension Type Safety","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Use generic constraints to make sure your APIs only ever receive the right kind of quantity."}],"role":"article"},"doc://Math/documentation/Math/PhysicalDimension/length":{"url":"\/documentation\/math\/physicaldimension\/length","fragments":[{"text":"static","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"var","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"length","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": `Self`","kind":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/length","type":"topic","title":"length","abstract":[{"text":"The dimension of length (SI base unit: meter).","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol"},"doc://Math/documentation/Math/LinearConverter":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"LinearConverter","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"tex